<template>
  <div class="rag-story">
    <p class="back-link"><router-link :to="{ path: '/projects', query: { to: 'pwcRag' } }">&larr; Back to projects</router-link></p>
    <h1 data-scroll-reveal>A Structure-Preserving RAG Ingestion Pipeline</h1>

    <article data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="110">
      <p class="lede">
        This project began with a practical requirement for a public-enquiry RAG system. When someone asks about a
        rule or service, retrieval needs to return the complete condition, qualification, or decision path that an
        answer depends on.
      </p>
      <p>
        The source documents made that difficult. Standard PDF parsing could split a merged table, break a table or
        paragraph at a page boundary, detach a note from the sentence it qualified, or leave a flowchart outside the
        searchable text. Once those relationships were lost during ingestion, retrieval could surface a plausible
        fragment with a missing condition or an incomplete decision path.
      </p>
      <p>
        I designed and built an ingestion pipeline that preserved or reconstructed those relationships before
        chunking. MinerU extracted structured text and tables. A small custom linker attached cross-page notes to
        the sentences they qualified, while Mermaid gave each flowchart a searchable text representation. The
        resulting chunks carried complete evidence into retrieval. The stages below follow a controlled test
        document through each transformation and explain the decisions behind the final pipeline.
      </p>

      <h2>From client constraints to a recommended architecture</h2>
      <p>
        Because this was consulting work, technical performance was one part of the decision. I compared possible
        architectures against the client&rsquo;s infrastructure, operating cost, implementation complexity, and the
        people who would need to run and maintain the system. One important constraint was a preference to keep the
        AI stack to a single model.
      </p>
      <p>
        That constraint shaped where AI was useful and where a deterministic method was stronger. I examined whether
        tables and flowcharts should share one visual-language route. The POC supported a leaner division of work.
        MinerU handled structured text and tables, one visual-language model handled visual material such as
        flowcharts, and explicit rules repaired cross-page references that needed predictable matching.
      </p>
      <p>
        The recommended solution therefore came from the requirements, architecture and cost analysis, and the POC
        results together. The deliverable covered the proposed stack, the role of each component, the tradeoffs
        behind the choice, the evidence from testing, and a practical path from prototype to implementation.
      </p>

      <div class="stage-list" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="120">
        <div
          v-for="(stage, index) in stages"
